INSERT INTO company_dossiers (ticker,status,description,lede,sells,revenue_model,opportunity,must_go_right,editor_note,bull_title,bull_json,bear_title,bear_json,thesis_breaker,financial_drivers_json,risks_json,monitor_json,questions_json,competitors,evidence_date,validated_at,published_at,updated_at) VALUES ('TSLA','published','Electric vehicles, energy storage, charging, services, autonomy, mobility, AI infrastructure, and robotics.','Tesla is a scaled vehicle and energy manufacturer using its installed fleet, direct distribution, software, charging network, battery supply chain, and manufacturing footprint to pursue autonomy and robotics. Owners must separate the economics already visible in automotive, energy, and services from the much larger value often assigned to Robotaxi, FSD, Cybercab, and Optimus.','Model 3, Y, S, X, Cybertruck and commercial vehicles; automotive software and connectivity; vehicle leasing, financing, insurance, service, used vehicles, and charging; Megapack, Powerwall, solar, and related services; and an expanding company-operated Robotaxi service. Optimus remains a development and manufacturing program rather than a material reported revenue line.','Vehicle and energy-product sales, regulatory credits, leasing, paid software and connectivity, services and used vehicles, charging, insurance, energy generation, and project or service contracts. Future mobility and robotics economics depend on utilization, take rate, safety, regulation, hardware life, service cost, and capital ownership.','Tesla can spread software, AI, charging, battery, power-electronics, and manufacturing investment across a large installed fleet and expanding energy base. If autonomy and robotics become safe, permitted, reliable, and economic, the company could add recurring, service-like profit pools to manufacturing revenue.','Vehicle demand and price realization must support adequate manufacturing returns; Energy must convert deployments into durable gross profit and cash flow; the 2026 capex step-up must earn attractive returns; FSD and Robotaxi must produce measurable paid usage with acceptable safety and regulation; and Optimus must advance from demonstrations to repeatable manufacturing and customer value.','The latest filing shows renewed scale but also the cost of the strategic expansion. Q2 2026 revenue rose 26% to $28.236 billion and common-stockholder net income was $1.114 billion, while first-half operating cash flow of $8.634 billion only modestly exceeded $8.282 billion of property-and-equipment purchases. Tesla expects more than $25 billion of 2026 capex, driven heavily by AI, data centers, manufacturing, and company-operated assets.','A self-funded industrial platform with several credible adjacencies','["Q2 revenue increased 26% to $28.236 billion as automotive, Energy, and services revenue all grew year over year; first-half revenue reached $50.623 billion.","Tesla delivered 480,126 vehicles and deployed 13.5 GWh of energy storage in Q2, demonstrating manufacturing and project scale across two large end markets.","First-half operating cash flow increased to $8.634 billion, and cash plus short-term investments remained $43.524 billion at June 30, providing unusual capacity to fund new products and infrastructure.","FSD subscriptions, Robotaxi operations, charging, services, insurance, and fleet software create multiple paths to increase lifetime revenue per vehicle beyond the original sale."]','The valuation depends on businesses whose economics are not yet proven','["Q2 automotive gross margin was 16.9%, below 17.2% a year earlier, while regulatory-credit revenue fell 67% to $146 million; volume alone does not guarantee stronger unit economics.","First-half property-and-equipment purchases more than doubled to $8.282 billion, and management expects 2026 capex above $25 billion before Robotaxi, Cybercab, AI compute, and Optimus reach mature returns.","Energy Q2 revenue increased to $3.139 billion, but segment gross profit fell to $640 million from $846 million and gross margin fell to 20.4% from 30.3%.","Autonomy, mobility, and robotics face technical, safety, liability, insurance, regulatory, service, and utilization risks; management forecasts and demonstrations are not substitutes for audited segment economics."]','The thesis breaks if the vehicle and Energy businesses cannot generate durable cash returns through the investment cycle while autonomy and robotics continue absorbing capital without achieving independently measurable safety, regulatory permission, utilization, and unit economics. A large addressable market would not compensate owners for structurally weak returns on the installed capital base.','[{"label":"Q2 REVENUE","value":"$28.236B · +26%","detail":"$20.516B automotive, $3.139B Energy, and $4.581B services and other; mix and gross profit matter more than deliveries alone"},{"label":"Q2 NET INCOME","value":"$1.114B","detail":"Common-stockholder GAAP net income fell 5% year over year even as revenue grew; reconcile non-operating items and stock-based compensation"},{"label":"AUTOMOTIVE MARGIN","value":"16.9%","detail":"Q2 total automotive gross margin versus 17.2% a year earlier; price, mix, credits, warranty, factories, and utilization drive the result"},{"label":"ENERGY","value":"13.5 GWh deployed","detail":"$3.139B Q2 segment revenue and $640M gross profit; deployments do not by themselves reveal margin, cash timing, or return on capital"},{"label":"LIQUIDITY","value":"$43.524B","detail":"Cash, cash equivalents, and short-term investments at June 30; provides resilience but should be assessed against commitments and planned investment"},{"label":"2026 INVESTMENT","value":">$25B capex expected","detail":"$8.282B spent in the first half, mainly for AI and operating infrastructure, factory expansion, machinery, and equipment"}]','[{"title":"Vehicle demand, price, and manufacturing returns","level":"High","text":"Tesla competes across price points and geographies while factories, batteries, tooling, service, inventory, and warranty create fixed and working-capital requirements. Incentives or financing can support deliveries while pressuring economic returns.","signal":"Orders, deliveries, inventory days, average selling price, incentives, leasing, automotive gross margin, and warranty cost"},{"title":"Autonomy safety, regulation, and liability","level":"High","text":"FSD remains supervised in many uses, while Robotaxi expansion requires operational safety, local permissions, insurance, teleoperations, charging, cleaning, maintenance, and public trust.","signal":"Paid miles, interventions, incidents, regulatory authorizations, recalls, claims, utilization, and contribution margin"},{"title":"Capital allocation and concurrent execution","level":"High","text":"Tesla is funding vehicles, batteries, materials, AI compute, custom silicon, Cybercab, Robotaxi infrastructure, charging, solar, and Optimus at once. Timing slips or low utilization can reduce returns across a very large program base.","signal":"Capex, operating cash flow, depreciation, project milestones, asset utilization, and return on invested capital"},{"title":"Supply chain, tariffs, and vertical integration","level":"High","text":"Cells, semiconductors, materials, power electronics, and regional trade rules affect both availability and cost. Internal production can improve control but adds ramp, yield, and underutilization risk.","signal":"Cell and pack capacity, material cost, tariffs, supplier concentration, factory yield, and localized sourcing"},{"title":"Governance, key-person, and related-party exposure","level":"High","text":"Strategy, brand, executive attention, compensation, board oversight, public communications, and transactions involving affiliated companies can materially affect owner outcomes.","signal":"Proxy disclosures, related-party transactions, board independence, executive retention, litigation, and shareholder dilution"}]','[{"title":"Automotive growth quality","text":"Track deliveries with realized pricing, mix, incentives, inventory, leasing, regulatory credits, warranty, and automotive gross profit—not unit volume alone.","impact":"NEUTRAL"},{"title":"Energy margin and cash conversion","text":"Compare storage deployments and revenue with segment gross profit, deferred revenue, receivables, project timing, capex, and service obligations.","impact":"BULL CASE"},{"title":"Robotaxi operating evidence","text":"Require paid rides and miles, utilization, intervention and incident data, city approvals, fleet ownership, operating cost, insurance, and contribution margin.","impact":"NEUTRAL"},{"title":"AI and manufacturing investment returns","text":"Measure the more-than-$25B 2026 capex plan against useful compute, factory throughput, Cybercab and Semi ramp, cash generation, and depreciation.","impact":"BEAR CASE"},{"title":"Optimus commercialization","text":"Track repeatable task performance, safety, production yield, bill of materials, external customer use, pricing, and disclosed revenue rather than prototype counts.","impact":"NEUTRAL"}]','["What normalized automotive gross margin and free-cash-flow yield are sustainable without regulatory credits or unusually favorable working capital?","What are Robotaxi revenue, paid utilization, direct operating cost, intervention rate, insurance loss experience, and cash return per vehicle?","How much of the more-than-$25B 2026 capex supports capacity with contracted or observable demand versus long-dated technology programs?","Can Energy sustain attractive gross margins and cash returns as Megapack capacity expands and tariff exposure changes?","When will Optimus have external customers, recognized revenue, repeatable task economics, and independently auditable safety and reliability evidence?","How are related-party transactions—including Tesla''s SpaceX investment and sales—reviewed, priced, and disclosed for minority shareholders?"]','No single peer spans vehicles, batteries, charging, storage, mobility, AI, and robotics. BYD, Toyota, GM, Volkswagen, Hyundai, and Rivian compete in vehicles; CATL and battery manufacturers influence cost and integration; Fluence and integrators compete in storage; Waymo and other mobility platforms compete in autonomy; robotics companies compete with Optimus.','2026-08-01',CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP) ON CONFLICT(ticker) DO UPDATE SET status='published',description=excluded.description,lede=excluded.lede,sells=excluded.sells,revenue_model=excluded.revenue_model,opportunity=excluded.opportunity,must_go_right=excluded.must_go_right,editor_note=excluded.editor_note,bull_title=excluded.bull_title,bull_json=excluded.bull_json,bear_title=excluded.bear_title,bear_json=excluded.bear_json,thesis_breaker=excluded.thesis_breaker,financial_drivers_json=excluded.financial_drivers_json,risks_json=excluded.risks_json,monitor_json=excluded.monitor_json,questions_json=excluded.questions_json,competitors=excluded.competitors,evidence_date=excluded.evidence_date,validated_at=CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,published_at=COALESCE(company_dossiers.published_at,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP),updated_at=CURRENT_TIMESTAMP;
